WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.030
Before a review cycle closes, managers often meet to reconcile their draft ratings.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:10.430
These calibration sessions are seldom checked against anything but their own minutes.

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:14.100
This paper asks if a session can be scored on a shared scale,

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.700
and if that score tracks anything beyond itself.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:20.650
Our index places each session at one of five levels.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:26.010
They climb from anecdote, through forced ranking, evidence packets and cross-calibration,

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:27.680
to blind recalibration.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:31.500
Two coders scored a hundred and fifty-six sessions,

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:35.220
in fifty-two units of a professional-services firm,

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:36.640
over three review cycles,

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.050
and agreed closely.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:44.887
We had an outside panel re-score the files of twelve employees per session,

00:00:44.887 --> 00:00:48.695
blind to the original ratings and the level.

00:00:48.695 --> 00:00:51.645
The median session ran twenty-four minutes at level one,

00:00:51.745 --> 00:00:53.464
and a hundred and eight at level five,

00:00:53.664 --> 00:00:56.284
with sixteen pages of evidence per nominee.

00:00:57.084 --> 00:01:00.954
But the level barely tracked how close the ratings came to the panel's scores.

00:01:01.154 --> 00:01:03.264
The correlation was point zero nine,

00:01:03.364 --> 00:01:05.154
too weak to tell apart from none.

00:01:05.954 --> 00:01:10.034
Meanwhile, the firm began setting each unit's bonus pool by its level alone.

00:01:10.234 --> 00:01:12.284
Point eight five times the base at level one,

00:01:12.384 --> 00:01:14.294
up to one point one five at level five.

00:01:15.094 --> 00:01:17.284
The schedule went in because the index looked right,

00:01:17.384 --> 00:01:19.034
before anyone had checked what it tracked.

00:01:19.834 --> 00:01:22.524
The index behaved exactly as an index should.

00:01:22.824 --> 00:01:26.784
What an organisation then does with that behaviour is a separate question,

00:01:26.884 --> 00:01:29.134
and one the Lab is glad to see asked out loud.
